    <div class="blog-post__content">
      <p>My AI knows my resting heart rate, my HRV, my sleep score, and my training load history. It told me this morning:</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>"You're NOT detrained. CTL of 29 is solid — not your peak of 37, but not a rebuild situation either."</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>Then it checked my recovery metrics — resting HR of 49, HRV of 53, sleep score of 94 — and said:</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>"Your body is in great shape for intervals. Go all-out tomorrow. Just don't stack multiple high-load days without a recovery day."</p>
      </blockquote>

      <div class="blog-post__inline-image">
        <img src="/images/blog-ai-coach-1.png" alt="AI analyzing training load, CTL, ATL, form, and recovery metrics" loading="lazy">
      </div>

      <h2>This isn't a fitness app</h2>

      <p>This is my AI assistant — the same one that reads my emails, signs my contracts, and books my Pilates classes. It just happens to know my training data too.</p>

      <p>How? Because it has access to my health data. It pulls my training metrics, understands the context (what I've been training for, when my next event is), and gives me <em>personalized</em> advice — not generic "get more sleep" tips.</p>

      <div class="blog-post__inline-image">
        <img src="/images/blog-ai-coach-2.png" alt="AI recommending interval training with load history analysis" loading="lazy">
      </div>

      <h2>One AI, every part of my life</h2>

      <p>The point isn't that AI can be a fitness coach. The point is that <strong>one assistant</strong> handles everything:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Work</strong> — reads my emails, drafts replies, signs contracts, manages my calendar</li>
        <li><strong>Life</strong> — books Pilates, makes reservations, tracks flights</li>
        <li><strong>Health</strong> — analyzes my training load, recovery metrics, and recommends workouts</li>
        <li><strong>Finance</strong> — sends me daily market briefings, stock price alerts, commodity tracking</li>
      </ul>

      <p>No app switching. No data silos. No separate logins for 10 different tools.</p>

      <div class="blog-post__inline-image">
        <img src="/images/blog-ai-coach-3.png" alt="Training load chart showing fitness, fatigue, and form over 5 weeks" loading="lazy">
      </div>

      <h2>The difference between 10 apps and 1 assistant</h2>

      <p>I used to have a training app, an email app, a calendar app, a note-taking app, a finance app, a booking app... Each one isolated. None of them connected.</p>

      <p>Now I have one AI that connects them all. When my AI tells me to go all-out tomorrow, it already knows I don't have early meetings. When it books my Pilates class, it already knows my training load that day.</p>

      <p>That context is what makes it different. Not smarter algorithms — just <em>more context</em> about your life.</p>

      <h2>How it works</h2>

      <p>The setup is simple:</p>

      <div class="highlight-box">
        <p><strong>1. Connect your data</strong> — give your AI access to the apps and data sources you already use (email, calendar, health, fitness)</p>
        <p><strong>2. Tell it what you need</strong> — in plain language, describe your goals, preferences, and routines</p>
        <p><strong>3. It learns and adapts</strong> — every interaction makes it better. It remembers your patterns and builds context over time</p>
      </div>

      <p>No coding. No complex setup. Just a conversation.</p>

      <h2>The point</h2>

      <p>An AI assistant isn't a fitness tracker, an email client, or a calendar. It's <em>one thing</em> that understands all of them together — and uses that understanding to actually help you, across every part of your life.</p>

      <p>That's what we set up for people. Not a tool you have to learn — an assistant that learns you.</p>
